    causes: [
      { name: 'Frost / ice buildup around the icemaker (the common Samsung issue)', why: 'On many Samsung French-door models, warm humid air reaches the icemaker compartment and slowly builds frost around the auger and sensors until it can no longer make or drop ice. This is the single most common Samsung ice-maker complaint.', diy: 'Empty and remove the ice bucket, then run the forced defrost or simply power the fridge off for 24 hours with the freezer emptied to fully melt the frost. If it works after that but returns in a few weeks, the compartment isn\'t sealing — that\'s a tech repair.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Water filter overdue', why: 'A clogged filter starves the icemaker of water. Samsung recommends changing it about every 6 months; past that, ice slows or stops.', diy: 'Replace the water filter and run a few dispenses to clear air, then give it 24 hours to make a batch.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Freezer not cold enough / wrong temp', why: 'The icemaker won\'t cycle reliably unless the freezer holds around 0°F. A freezer running warm makes little or no ice.', diy: 'Set the freezer to 0°F, make sure vents aren\'t blocked by food, and confirm the door seals fully.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Kinked or frozen water line', why: 'The thin water line feeding the icemaker can kink behind the fridge or freeze solid, cutting off supply.', diy: 'Check that the line behind the fridge isn\'t pinched. A frozen line usually clears during the 24-hour power-off defrost above.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Failed icemaker assembly or water inlet valve', why: 'When resets and defrosts stop holding, the icemaker module itself, its sensor, or the water inlet valve has failed and needs replacing — a common, well-understood Samsung repair.', diy: 'This is the tech diagnosis: we confirm whether it\'s the assembly, the valve, or a sealing issue in the compartment and replace the right part.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
    ],
    repairReplace: 'Repair — a Samsung refrigerator is worth fixing, and ice-maker assemblies and inlet valves are affordable parts. The key is a tech who knows the Samsung frosting pattern and addresses the sealing/airflow cause, not just the symptom, so it doesn\'t come right back.',
