    causes: [
      { name: 'Clogged dryer vent (the #1 cause)', why: 'Lint builds up in the vent hose and the duct running outside until hot, moist air can\'t escape — so clothes stay damp and dry times double or triple. It\'s also the leading cause of dryer fires (the USFA/CSIA recommend service yearly).', diy: 'Disconnect and clean the flexible vent hose behind the dryer, and check that the flap on the outside wall opens when the dryer runs. If the run is long or you can\'t reach it, we clean the full line — we\'re CSIA C-DET certified.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Clogged lint screen (even if it looks clean)', why: 'The lint screen catches lint, but fabric softener and dryer sheets leave an invisible film that blocks airflow through the mesh even when it looks empty.', diy: 'Pull the screen, and once a month scrub it under water with a little dish soap and a soft brush — if water pools on the mesh instead of flowing through, it was clogged.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Overloading', why: 'A packed drum leaves no room for air to tumble through the clothes, so nothing dries evenly. Big comforters are the classic offender.', diy: 'Dry smaller loads — the drum should be no more than about three-quarters full.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Partial heat loss (weak element or thermostat)', why: 'A heating element that\'s partially failed, or a cycling thermostat that\'s dropping heat early, produces some warmth but not enough to dry in a normal cycle.', diy: 'If the vent and lint screen are clear and it still runs long, the heat side needs a tech to test the element and thermostats.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Failed moisture sensor', why: 'Auto-dry cycles use a moisture sensor to decide when clothes are dry. A dirty or failed sensor can end the cycle early, leaving clothes damp.', diy: 'Try a Timed Dry cycle instead of Auto — if that dries fine, the moisture sensor is the culprit (dryer-sheet residue on the sensor bars can also cause it; wipe them).', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
    ],
    repairReplace: 'Almost always repair — and often the fix is a vent cleaning, not a part at all. Elements and thermostats are affordable when needed. A dryer that takes forever is wasting energy and building fire risk, so it\'s worth handling promptly.',
