    metaTitle: 'Whirlpool Dryer AF Code? Restricted Airflow — Fix From a Real Tech',
    metaDesc: 'AF on a Whirlpool dryer means restricted airflow — the exhaust vent is clogged. It\'s a fire risk and makes clothes take forever. Here\'s what to do.',
    intro: 'AF on a Whirlpool dryer stands for "Air Flow" — the dryer detected that its exhaust is restricted and warned you. It\'s not a broken part; it\'s the dryer telling you the vent path is choked with lint. That matters for two reasons: clothes take forever to dry, and a clogged dryer vent is the leading cause of dryer fires. Here\'s what it means and how to clear it.',
    safety: '⚠️ Restricted airflow is a real fire hazard — lint is highly flammable and heat backs up when it can\'t escape. Clean the lint path before running more long loads. This is a code worth acting on quickly.',
    causes: [
      { name: 'Clogged lint screen or vent run', why: 'The most common cause. Lint builds up in the screen and, over time, packs the flexible duct and the rigid vent line running to the outside of the house — choking airflow and triggering AF.', diy: 'Clean the lint screen every load. Then pull the dryer out and clean the duct behind it. The full vent run to the exterior usually needs a proper cleaning — that\'s our dryer-vent cleaning service, and it clears the code and the fire risk.', difficulty: 'Moderate' },
      { name: 'Crushed or kinked transition hose', why: 'The flexible hose behind the dryer gets crushed when the dryer is pushed back against the wall, strangling airflow even if the rest of the run is clean.', diy: 'Pull the dryer out a few inches and make sure the hose behind it isn\'t crushed flat or kinked. Give it room.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Blocked exterior vent flap', why: 'The vent hood outside can clog with lint or its flap can stick shut (or get blocked by a bird nest), trapping the air.', diy: 'Go outside, find the dryer vent hood, and check that the flap opens freely and isn\'t packed with lint or debris.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Failing blower wheel', why: 'If the whole vent path is clean but AF persists, the blower wheel that moves the air may be cracked or clogged and not pushing enough exhaust.', diy: 'If cleaning the vent doesn\'t clear AF, the blower is a tech diagnosis and repair.', difficulty: 'Pro' },
    ],
    repairReplace: 'This is usually a vent-cleaning job, not a dryer repair — the dryer is working correctly by warning you. A professional vent cleaning clears AF, restores drying speed, and removes the fire risk. If it\'s the blower wheel, that\'s still an affordable part.',
    faqs: [
      { q: 'How do I fix the AF code on my Whirlpool dryer?', a: 'Clean the lint screen, pull the dryer out and clear the duct behind it, and check the exterior vent flap. The full vent run to the outside usually needs a professional cleaning to fully clear AF — it\'s an airflow problem, not a broken part.' },
      { q: 'Is the AF code dangerous?', a: 'It\'s a warning that your dryer\'s exhaust is restricted, which is a genuine fire risk — clogged vents are the top cause of dryer fires. Clear the vent promptly, and don\'t keep running long loads until you do.' },
      { q: 'Why does my Whirlpool dryer take forever to dry with an AF code?', a: 'Restricted airflow means moist air can\'t leave the drum, so clothes stay damp and cycles run long. Clearing the vent restores normal drying — that\'s exactly what our dryer-vent cleaning covers.' },
    ],
