    question: 'My refrigerator runs constantly and never shuts off — what do I do?',
    metaTitle: 'Refrigerator Running Constantly? Causes & Fixes From a Real Technician',
    metaDesc: 'A fridge that never shuts off is usually dirty condenser coils, bad door seals, or a defrost/fan issue. Here\'s what to check before you call a tech.',
    intro: 'A refrigerator that runs nonstop is working too hard to hold its temperature — and that runs up your power bill and wears the compressor. Usually it\'s something making the fridge lose cold or struggle to make it, and several causes you can check yourself. Here\'s the order a tech runs through them, cheapest first.',
    safety: 'Low-risk checks. If you pull the fridge out to reach the coils or the plug, be gentle with the water line to the icemaker/dispenser.',
    causes: [
      { name: 'Dirty condenser coils (most common)', why: 'The coils that release heat — usually underneath or on the back — clog with dust and pet hair. Choked coils can\'t shed heat, so the fridge runs constantly trying to cool.', diy: 'Unplug the fridge, find the coils (behind the front kick plate or on the back), and vacuum/brush them clean. This alone fixes a large share of "runs all the time" calls and is free.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Door seals leaking or door left ajar', why: 'Worn or dirty door gaskets let cold leak out, so the fridge never catches up. A drawer or item blocking the door from sealing does the same.', diy: 'Wipe the gaskets clean and check the seal with a dollar bill — close it in the door; if it slides out easily, the seal is weak. Make sure nothing blocks the door from closing fully.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Warm room, packed fridge, or high setting', why: 'A hot kitchen, an overstuffed fridge blocking airflow, or the temperature set colder than needed all make it run more.', diy: 'Leave room for air to circulate around the vents inside, and set the fridge around 37°F / freezer 0°F rather than max cold.', difficulty: 'Easy' },
      { name: 'Defrost or fan problem / failing part', why: 'A frost buildup on the evaporator (from a defrost fault) or a failing condenser/evaporator fan makes cooling inefficient, so the compressor runs nonstop. Less often, the compressor or control is weakening.', diy: 'If the coils are clean, seals are good, and it still never stops — especially with frost buildup or a fridge that\'s slightly warm — that\'s a tech diagnosis.', difficulty: 'Pro' },
    ],
    repairReplace: 'Usually repair — most constant-running is free (clean the coils, fix the seal) or an affordable fan/defrost part. Replacing the fridge only makes sense if it\'s a failing sealed system on an older unit, which a tech can confirm.',
